  m_Script: {fileID: 11500000, guid: 42a7ef911f4c60e408989a759d2b9b18, type: 3}
  m_Name: PHP_LOOPS
  m_EditorClassIdentifier: Assembly-CSharp::CodeEscape.Programming.LessonData
  id: PHP_LOOPS
  language: 2
  conceptTitle: 'Loops: the for loop'
  symbol: for
  relatedQuestionIds:
  - PHP_07_LOOPS
  - HZ_PHP_04_LOOPS
  topics:
  - Loops
  definition: A loop repeats a block of code while a condition stays true.
  howItWorks: 'A PHP for loop has three parts inside round brackets, separated by
    semicolons: a start (for example $i = 0), a condition checked before every run
    ($i < 3), and a step that runs after every run ($i++ adds 1). The code to repeat
    goes inside curly braces { }.'
  exampleCode: "<?php\nfor ($n = 2; $n <= 6; $n += 2) {\n    echo $n . \" \";\n}\n?>"
  exampleExplanation: $n starts at 2. Each time, the loop checks $n <= 6, outputs
    $n and a space, then adds 2. It outputs 2 4 6 and stops when $n becomes 8, because
    8 <= 6 is false.
  tip: $i++ is a short way of writing $i = $i + 1.
